<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Haliartus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/395-bce">395 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliartus">Haliartus</a> was fought in 395 BCE outside the Boeotian city of Haliartus at the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corinthian-war">Corinthian War</a>. The Spartan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> attacked the city before the main Spartan army under King Pausanias arrived, and was killed when the Thebans and the defenders sallied out. His death was a serious blow to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, and Pausanias withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a> under truce and was later exiled.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In 396 or 395 BCE Timocrates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodes">Rhodes</a>, an envoy of the Persian satrap Pharnabazus, arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and promised Persian money and support to the leading states if they would make war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>. Sparta's aggressive and unilateral conduct had angered many of its allies, and the offer was enough to bring several states, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> above all, to war.</p><p>The Thebans chose to start the war indirectly. They persuaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locrians">Locrians</a> to raid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocis">Phocis</a>, an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> was bound to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/locris">Locris</a>, and Phocis appealed to Sparta. The Spartans saw a chance to discipline the restive Thebans and prepared a major campaign, while Thebes sought help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and a perpetual alliance was concluded between the Athenians and the Boeotians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Spartan plan called for two armies to meet at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haliartus">Haliartus</a> for a combined attack: one under King Pausanias of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agiad-dynasty">Agiad dynasty</a> with Spartan and Peloponnesian troops, the other under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> with Phocians and allies from north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. Pausanias lingered for several days in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, and Lysander reached Haliartus first. Unwilling to wait, he tried to take the city by subversion and, when that failed, assaulted its walls.</p><p>A large Theban force nearby, perhaps unknown to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>, hurried to help the defenders. In the fighting beneath the walls Lysander's army was routed and he was killed. The Thebans pursued too far into rough and steep ground, where the fleeing troops turned and drove them back with heavy losses. The next day Lysander's army broke up, each contingent going home.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>When Pausanias arrived days later, the Thebans let him recover the bodies only if he left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boeotia">Boeotia</a>, and he agreed. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a>'s faction put him on trial for arriving late and not attacking, and expecting to be condemned to death, he went into exile. With Lysander dead and Pausanias gone, Agesilaus was left to direct Spartan policy for years. The war that began here lasted until 387 BCE, with battles at Nemea and Coronea the next year.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Haliartus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Corinthian War Boeotian forces killed the Spartan commander Lysander at the <b>Battle of Haliartus</b> in 395 BCE and fought Agesilaus II at the Battle of Coronea in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>They stood their ground against the Spartans at the <b>Battle of Haliartus</b> in 395 BCE and the Battle of Coronea in 394 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li></ul></section>
